{
  "groups": [
    {
      "heading": "[508] config: hosts was REJECTED, now ACCEPTED",
      "verdict": "MATTERS",
      "why": "The restored zone-index branch is much wider than what zod 3 accepted, so this is a new hole, not the intended compatibility shim. Probed the raw oracle library: zod3's z.string().ip() accepts a %zone suffix ONLY on the fe80 link-local compressed form (\"fe80::1%eth0\" -> true, \"fe80::abcd:1234%eth0\" -> true) and rejects it everywhere else (\"3d62::9037%eth0\", \"::1%eth0\", \"::%1\", \"2001:db8::1%eth0\", \"fec0::1%eth0\", \"ff02::1%eth0\", \"fe80:0:0:0:0:0:0:1%eth0\" -> all false). src/schemas.ts's zonedIpv6 instead accepts any address z.ipv6() likes plus any non-empty zone string, which is exactly why all eight fuzzer examples (5ac3::de2a%wlan0, 2459::52d2%lo0, 7e60::16eb%25, 2001:db8::ff00:42:8329%wlan0, ...) flipped to ACCEPTED - I reproduced each through the merged build. It also newly accepts junk zones: \"fe80::1%eth0%bad\", \"fe80::1%eth0 \" (trailing space), \"fe80::1%../../etc\", \"fe80::1%localhost:8080\", \"::%0\" - all rejected by zod3, all accepted now. review/corpus-diffs.md signed off the ip fix on the explicit claim that the union 'reproduces exactly the set zod 3 matched ... nothing new is let in'; that claim is factually wrong, so the sign-off does not cover this. hosts entries are connection targets, so unvalidated zone text (whitespace, a second '%', path-traversal-looking strings) now flows downstream. Action: tighten zonedIpv6 to the form zod3 actually matched (fe80 link-local, zone restricted to something like [0-9A-Za-z._-]+ / %25-escaped), or get an explicit, documented decision to widen. Judgement: the security framing is mine; the acceptance-set facts are probed."
    },
    {
      "heading": "[463] config: timeoutMs was ACCEPTED, now REJECTED",
      "verdict": "MATTERS",
      "why": "Real, undocumented narrowing that nobody has signed off. Probed: timeoutMs = Infinity is accepted by the oracle and rejected by the merged build ('Invalid input: expected number, received Infinity') - zod3's z.number() allowed non-finite values, zod 4's does not. (The group's examples are all Infinity; a probe that printed inputs via JSON.stringify made some of these look like null.) Finite behaviour is unchanged: 1, 0.5, 5000, 1e+308 identical, NaN and -Infinity rejected by both, and an omitted field still defaults to 5000 in both builds. review/corpus-diffs.md covers ip, name and age only and lists nothing about non-finite numbers (the 400-request corpus contained no such value), so this change is outside the human's sign-off. Judgement: unlike the age probes, 'Infinity' is an idiomatic 'never time out' sentinel in JS and Config is assembled in code (JSON cannot express Infinity), so a deployment that passes it would now fail config validation at startup rather than run. That is a product/ops call a human should make and record - either ACCEPT NEW with a note, or accept Infinity explicitly."
    },
    {
      "heading": "[357] signup: ip was REJECTED, now ACCEPTED",
      "verdict": "MATTERS",
      "why": "Same root cause as group [508], on the Signup.ip field instead of Config.hosts (both use the shared ipAddress union). Probed each example through both builds: \"3d62::9037%1\", \"::%1\", \"2001:db8::ff00:42:8329%25\", \"b87e:...%en0\", \"dafa::c206%eth0\" are rejected by the zod3 oracle and accepted now, because zod3 only allowed a %zone on the fe80 link-local form while zonedIpv6 allows any ipv6 + any non-empty zone (confirmed directly against the raw zod3 library). Newly accepted junk also lands here: \"fe80::1%eth0 \", \"fe80::1%eth0%bad\", \"::%anything-at-all\", \"fe80::1%../../etc\". The signed-off review asserted the union lets in nothing new, which the probe contradicts, so a human must either tighten zonedIpv6 or consciously accept the wider set. Fixing it once in ipAddress resolves [508], [357] and most of [88]/[47]."
    },
    {
      "heading": "[324] signup: age was ACCEPTED, now REJECTED",
      "verdict": "NOISE",
      "why": "This is precisely the change a human already reviewed and accepted: review/corpus-diffs.md section '[2] age - was ACCEPTED, now REJECTED' documents zod 4's .int() meaning *safe* integer with a +/-MAX_SAFE_INTEGER bound, and the sign-off line reads 'ACCEPT NEW on age (unsafe integers now rejected) - agreed', with no code change intended. Probed the boundary and the scope matches exactly: 13, 130, 9007199254740991 accepted by both; 12 and 1.5 rejected by both; only 9007199254740992 and above (and 1e+21, 1e+30, 1.797e+308) differ. Every example in this group is above 2^53, i.e. not a representable age and already lossy as a float. No action needed before shipping; the suggested follow-up (an explicit .max(130)) is already recorded as out of scope for this PR."
    },
    {
      "heading": "[88] signup: rejected by both, but different fields reported",
      "verdict": "NOISE",
      "why": "Derivative of groups [357] and [324], with no independent behaviour change: the API verdict is 'rejected' in both builds for every example, only the issue list differs. I checked all eight examples and each delta is exactly 'ip disappears' (zoned address now accepted, group [357]) and/or 'age appears' (unsafe integer now rejected, group [324]); nothing else moves. Reproduced the mechanism directly: 'bad email + zoned ip' gives zod3 [email,ip] vs now [email]; 'bad email + unsafe age' gives zod3 [email] vs now [age,email]; 'everything bad' differs only by the added age. Error *wording* is deliberately not compared by corpus/canon.js, and a caller still gets the same 400 either way. Judgement: no separate action - these entries shrink automatically once the ip refine from [357] is decided, so tracking them as their own bug would be double-counting."
    },
    {
      "heading": "[47] config: rejected by both, but different fields reported",
      "verdict": "NOISE",
      "why": "Same reasoning as [88], on the Config schema: rejected by both builds in every example, so the externally visible outcome is unchanged; only the reported field set moves. Each delta traces to one of the two root causes already flagged: 'hosts.N' dropping out is the zone widening from group [508], and 'timeoutMs' appearing is the Infinity narrowing from group [463]. Reproduced: {hosts:['nope','3d62::9037%1'], timeoutMs:Infinity} -> zod3 [hosts.0,hosts.1] vs now [hosts.0,timeoutMs]; {hosts:[], timeoutMs:Infinity} -> zod3 [hosts] vs now [hosts,timeoutMs]; {hosts:['nope'], timeoutMs:-1} -> identical [hosts.0,timeoutMs] in both. No new acceptance and no new rejection of a whole config here, so nothing to act on beyond fixing [508] and deciding [463]."
    },
    {
      "heading": "[8] signup: website accepted, but the PARSED VALUE changed",
      "verdict": "NOISE",
      "why": "Judgement call, but no action needed. Probed the scope of zod 4's z.url() output change: it strips leading/trailing whitespace and removes embedded tab/newline (WHATWG URL sanitisation) - ' https://example.com' and 'https://example.com\\n' lose the whitespace, and 'https://exa\\tmple.com' becomes 'https://example.com'. It does nothing else: case ('HTTPS://Example.COM/A'), explicit port, '/a/../b' path, query order, fragment, userinfo, IDN host and '%7E' percent-encoding all come back byte-for-byte identical in both builds, and acceptance/rejection of URLs is unchanged ('url no scheme' rejected by both). So the only affected inputs are ones containing whitespace or control characters, which zod3 stored raw; the new stored value is the one any URL consumer (fetch/browser) would actually resolve, and dropping stray CR/LF out of a persisted URL is the safer of the two behaviours. 8 of 10000 inputs, all synthetic whitespace variants, and the production corpus contained none. Worth one line in the release notes since it is an output change the signed-off review does not mention, but it needs no code change before shipping."
    }
  ]
}

